arXiv:2509.06278v1 [cs.AI] 08 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">TableMind: An Autonomous Programmatic Agent for Tool-Augmented Table Reasoning</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_doi"><span class="ltx_note_name">DOI: </span><a href="https://doi.org/XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX">XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX</a></span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_conference"><span class="ltx_note_name">Conference: </span>Make sure to enter the correct
conference title from your rights confirmation email; June 03–05,
2018; Woodstock, NY</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_isbn"><span class="ltx_note_name">ISBN: </span>978-1-4503-XXXX-X/2018/06</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Chuang Jiang, Mingyue Cheng<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>, Xiaoyu Tao, Qingyang Mao, Jie Ouyang, Qi Liu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">State Key Laboratory of Cognitive Intelligence, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:jiangchuang,%20txytiny,%20maoqy0503,%20ouyang_jie@mail.ustc.edu.cn,mycheng,%20qiliuql@ustc.edu.cn">jiangchuang, txytiny, maoqy0503, ouyang_jie@mail.ustc.edu.cn,mycheng, qiliuql@ustc.edu.cn</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2018</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Table reasoning is crucial for leveraging structured data in domains such as finance, healthcare, and scientific research. While large language models (LLMs) show promise in multi-step reasoning, purely text-based methods often struggle with the complex numerical computations and fine-grained operations inherently required in this task. Tool-integrated reasoning improves computational accuracy via explicit code execution, yet existing systems frequently rely on rigid patterns, supervised imitation, and lack true autonomous adaptability. In this paper, we present TableMind, an LLM-driven table reasoning agent that (i) autonomously performs multi-turn tool invocation, (ii) writes and executes data-analyzing code in a secure sandbox environment for data analysis and precise numerical reasoning, and (iii) exhibits high-level capabilities such as planning and self-reflection to adapt strategies. To realize these capabilities, we adopt a two-stage fine-tuning paradigm built on top of a powerful pre-trained language model: supervised fine-tuning on high-quality reasoning trajectories to establish effective tool usage patterns, followed by reinforcement fine-tuning to optimize multi-objective strategies. In particular, we propose Rank-Aware Policy Optimization (RAPO), which increases the update weight of high-quality trajectories when their output probabilities are lower than those of low-quality ones, thereby guiding the model more consistently toward better and more accurate answers. Extensive experiments on several mainstream benchmarks demonstrate that TableMind achieves superior performance compared to competitive baselines, yielding substantial gains in both reasoning accuracy and computational precision.
To ensure the reproducibility of our results, we have made our experiment codes publicly available at <a href="https://github.com/lennendd/TableMind" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/lennendd/TableMind</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Table reasoning, Programmatic Agent, LLMs
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06278v1/overView.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/114;" width="476" height="114" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>TableMind mimics the human chain of thought by using a multi-turn plan-action-reflect loop to solve table tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tabular data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hollmann et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite> serves as a cornerstone for structured information across diverse domains, from scientific research<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and clinical reporting<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pampari et al., 2018</a>)</cite> to financial analysis<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. The combination of this data’s widespread use and recent breakthroughs in artificial intelligence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> has sparked significant academic and industrial interest in automated table reasoning<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Effectively solving foundational tasks like table question answering<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pasupat and Liang, 2015</a>)</cite> or table fact verification<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aly et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2019</a>)</cite> over tables presents a complex challenge, as it requires a combination of different skills: the precise retrieval of specific facts, robust natural language understanding, and accurate numerical computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The recent evolution of LLMs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhai, 2024</a>)</cite> has marked a paradigm shift in natural language processing. Techniques like Chain-of-Thought<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite> prompting have unlock powerful multistep reasoning abilities. However, these models falter when confronted with quantitative tasks grounded in structured data, as their internal mechanisms for numerical computation and symbolic logic are inherently limited. To address this, research has increasingly focused on frameworks in which LLMs act as a reasoning engine to generate code for external execution. This synergy between the high-level reasoning of an LLM and the precise computation of a code environment has dramatically improved the accuracy of table reasoning. To structure this synergy, many state-of-the-art methods have adopted workflow-driven methodologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, such as those following a plan-then-execute paradigm. These frameworks impose a structured control-flow on the problem-solving process, guiding the model through a pre-defined sequence of steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, the rigidity of these predefined workflows presents three potential constraints, hindering the development of truly autonomous reasoning. Firstly, their reliance on fixed templates or analysis patterns restricts flexibility when encountering diverse or unseen table structures and query types. Secondly, models trained via supervised imitation to mimic these fixed reasoning trajectories are often restricted in their ability to explore novel or more efficient strategies autonomously. Thirdly, and most critically, these systems lack a mechanism for iterative self-correction; they tend to follow a linear execution path and struggle to adapt when faced with unexpected execution errors or intermediate results. Metaphorically speaking, these methods behave more like clerks following a fixed checklist—the workflow—rather than expert analysts who can improvise, learn from mistakes, and refine their approach on the fly. This distinction highlights the need to move from structured workflows to dynamic agents<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To bridge this gap, we propose TableMind, a novel programmatic agent for tool-augmented table reasoning that achieves autonomous and adaptive problem-solving. At its core, TableMind operates in an iterative plan-action-reflect loop. It first decomposes a complex problem into a sequence of executable steps. It then generates and runs Python code to perform each step. Finally, it critically evaluates the execution feedback—whether a successful output or an error message—to reassess its progress and dynamically adjust its subsequent actions. This iterative cycle empowers the agent to solve tasks with the flexibility and resilience of a human data analyst, enabling it to autonomously navigate complex reasoning paths and recover from errors.
To train TableMind, we propose a two-stage optimization framework that enables the agent to evolve from imitation learning to strategic autonomy. The first SFT stage, injects an inductive bias for tool use, allowing the model to master essential syntax and common calling patterns. The second stage leverages Reinforcement Fine-tuning (RFT) to transition the agent from knowing how to use tools to understanding when, why, and how to use them optimally. To guide this process effectively, we design a fine-grained, multi-objective reward function and introduce RAPO, a novel algorithm that weights learning based on trajectory quality to improve exploration efficiency in complex strategy spaces. Through this framework, TableMind demonstrates two key advantages. First, by reflecting on execution outcomes, it can dynamically adjust its plan, achieving high flexibility. Second, its self-correction loop allows the agent to recover from intermediate failures, ensuring more reliable final outputs. Extensive experiments on multiple benchmark datasets show that TableMind achieves significant performance gains over existing baselines. Comprehensive analyses further highlight the framework’s effectiveness, adaptability, and ability to generate logical and coherent reasoning paths.To summarize, our main contributions can be listed as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose TableMind, a novel agent framework for table reasoning that seamlessly integrates autonomous planning, precise tool execution, and reflective adaptation, enabling flexible and robust problem-solving.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a two-stage training paradigm that effectively combines SFT for foundational knowledge acquisition with RFT for advanced strategy optimization, further enhanced by a novel rank-aware policy optimization method.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive and rigorous experiments demonstrating that TableMind consistently achieves state-of-the-art performance, clearly showcasing its superior adaptability and advanced reasoning capabilities across diverse and challenging table reasoning tasks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table reasoning has been a prominent research area for the last decade. The rise of LLMs and agents has recently led to major breakthroughs in core components like natural language understanding and numerical computation. This section will focus on research related to table reasoning and LLM agents.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Table Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By presenting data in a structured format, tables serve as a powerful means for organizing, storing, and analyzing information.
Inspired by the success of Masked Language Modeling in BERT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koroteev, 2021</a>)</cite>, involves masking parts of the table. For instance, TaPas<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Herzig et al., 2020</a>)</cite> adopts this by requiring the model to reconstruct masked cells during pre-training. Pasta<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and TUTA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2021</a>)</cite> extend this concept by proposing to mask entire columns or segments of the table.
In contrast, TAPEX<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> pursues a different paradigm. It pre-trains an encoder-decoder model on a large, synthetic SQL dataset. This enables the model to function as a SQL executor, thereby achieving a deeper understanding of the tabular structure.
With the emergence of LLMs, the focus shifted toward general-purpose systems that can perform multiple table reasoning tasks without task-specific retraining. Models such as TableLLaMA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, TableLLM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and the TableGPT series<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zha et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2024</a>)</cite> adapt LLMs to structured data by combining language reasoning with table parsing, often achieving strong zero-shot and few-shot performance.
Another line of work emphasizes workflow-driven methods. Chain-of-Table<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite> follows a data-flow paradigm, updating the table after each reasoning step, while PoTable<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> adopts a plan-then-execute strategy, decoupling planning from execution through external tools.
Recent reinforcement learning frameworks have notably enhanced the reasoning capabilities of LLMs, as seen in DeepSeek-R1<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Extending this line, Table-R1<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> applies rule-based reinforcement learning to structured tables. Overall, language models have become central to table reasoning<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, underscoring their growing research significance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>LLM-based Agent</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models often struggle with complex reasoning or multi-step planning tasks when relying solely on purely text-based inference. To address these challenges, recent work has increasingly integrated external tool use and explicit planning strategies to augment model capabilities<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. For example, some methods adopt an iterative data-flow approach that continuously updates an internal state after each reasoning step, while others follow a plan-and-execute paradigm—first generating a detailed plan and then invoking external tools to carry it out. However, such rigid, predefined pipelines can significantly limit flexibility, motivating the development of more dynamic agent frameworks that plan and select tools on the fly. One such approach is ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which interleaves reasoning and action: the agent alternates between thinking and taking an action based on its current reasoning state, enabling continuous interaction with an external environment. Another example is CRITIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, where the agent learns via self-reflection by using external resources (e.g., search engines or knowledge bases) to validate and refine its decisions, effectively self-correcting mistakes. Building on these ideas, our work trains an LLM-based agent on a smaller-scale model to explore autonomous tool use. Despite progress in LLM agent research, the design of fully autonomous execution pipelines—such as enabling the agent to write and run code within a secure sandbox—remains largely unexplored.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first define the table reasoning problem and formalize its key components. We then present the core ideas and underlying principles of TableMind that guide our approach.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Task Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the task of table reasoning as a structured decision-making problem grounded in tool-augmented language modeling. Each instance is represented as a tuple <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,Q,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,Q,A)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a structured table, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is a natural language query (e.g., a question or a factual statement), and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding answer. Unlike conventional QA settings, table reasoning often involves multi-step computation, intermediate result verification, and error correction. Thus, the task requires not only semantic understanding of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, but also precise numerical reasoning, which may involve intermediate state tracking, external tool usage, and dynamic adjustment of the reasoning path. Formally, the goal is to learn a reasoning policy <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> that, given the initial inputs <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,Q)</annotation></semantics></math>, generates a sequence of intermediate reasoning steps <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, and produces a final answer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}=\mathcal{R}(\{s_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}=\mathcal{R}(\{s_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an aggregation or termination function, and each step <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> may involve code generation, execution, and feedback-based refinement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Design Principles</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models demonstrate strong capabilities in understanding natural language, making them valuable tools for the complex task of table reasoning. This task requires interpreting structured data, executing precise computations, and synthesizing intermediate results, often necessitating interaction with external tools to ensure accuracy. However, current research presents a distinct dilemma, forcing a choice between two insufficient paradigms.
On one hand, many existing approaches omit tool usage entirely, treating reasoning as a single-pass generation process. These methods lack mechanisms for external verification or self-correction, often failing on tasks that require complex computation. On the other hand, the few methods that do incorporate tool interaction are heavily reliant on massive, proprietary models. Their tool invocation patterns are often rigid and pre-defined, lacking the capacity for dynamic reflection, and their dependence on large-scale models creates significant barriers to computational cost and adaptability.
This landscape reveals a critical and unfilled research gap: the absence of systems that combine autonomous tool invocation and reflection with lightweight, accessible models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06278v1/frameDiagram.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>The overall training pipeline for TableMind. The process begins with Prompt Building and SFT to warm up the model by providing it with a strong initial policy. Subsequently, RFT with RAPO is applied to significantly enhance its generalization capability. The final model is then deployed for Inference and evaluated for accuracy.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>TableMind</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section describes the construction of the TableMind, which is trained using a two-stage process of Supervised Fine-tuning and Reinforcement Fine-tuning. This allows it to implement a plan-action-reflect loop for flexible and autonomous table reasoning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Overview</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TableMind consists of a two-stage training framework for table reasoning with LLMs. This framework leverages a structured prompt template to unify various table inputs and to teach the model how to understand task instructions.
The first SFT stage aims to equip the model with the foundational ability to invoke tools. We use examples that distill from expert models as training data. The second stage employs RFT to guide the model in autonomously exploring superior tool-use strategies and more complex reasoning paths. We designed a fine-grained reward function tailored for table reasoning and introduced the RAPO method. By ranking and weighting reasoning trajectories of varying quality, RAPO efficiently optimizes decision sequences to strike a balance between final answer accuracy. An overview of the framework is provided in Figure  <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2. Design Principles ‣ 3. Preliminaries ‣ TableMind: An Autonomous Programmatic Agent for Tool-Augmented Table Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Prompt Template</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a structured prompt template to ensure consistent and accurate reasoning from the Large Language Model. Our prompt is constructed by combining two main components: a general Instruction block and a problem-specific table task block. The Instruction component defines the model’s persona and core operational methodology. It assigns the role of an expert AI data analyst and mandates a specific reasoning process: analyzing the problem, planning a strategy, executing code for data manipulation and reflecting the output. The table task block component provides the concrete details for each specific problem instance. It consists of the table content and the corresponding table question.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Supervised Fine-tuning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying reinforcement learning directly to complex tabular reasoning poses significant challenges—specifically, an overly broad search space and slow convergence. To address these issues, training begins with an essential SFT stage. This stage serves as a warm-up phase, aligning the model’s output format, enabling it to master the basic syntax of the plan–action–reflect cycle, and establishing an initial, reasonably stable policy for generating valid and logically coherent code. This design alleviates the instability associated with starting from a cold policy that lacks basic tool-use capabilities.
We construct a high-quality dataset of reasoning trajectories for SFT using an iterative, multi-turn framework. At its core, this framework employs a knowledge distillation<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al., 2015</a>)</cite> approach, aiming to convert the programmatic knowledge and reasoning processes of an expert model into structured training samples. First, we prompt the expert model to generate a plan and a corresponding initial executable action-a code snippet. This action is then executed in a local environment, and the resulting output or error message is returned to the model as an observation. Upon receiving this feedback, the model enters a reflection phase to evaluate the outcome of the preceding action, based on which it refines its plan and generates the next action. This interactive loop of plan-action-reflect continues until the task is completed and a final answer is obtained. All collected trajectories undergo a rigorous calibration process. We compare the final output of each trajectory against a predefined ground-truth answer, retaining only those that accurately lead to the correct solution. This filtering ensures that the final dataset comprises exclusively high-quality, validated reasoning paths. After collecting the training data, we fine-tune the model using SFT with a cross-entropy loss function. The initial SFT phase serves as a crucial preparatory step, lowering the overhead for the subsequent RFT. While this stage successfully imparts foundational instruction-following skills and nascent reasoning abilities, the reasoning itself is not yet robust. It largely reflects a form of pattern memorization from the training data, exhibiting poor generalization to out-of-distribution tasks. Therefore, this SFT-induced reasoning can be characterized as a form of memory that lacks the ability to generalize, necessitating further refinement through RFT.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Reinforcement Fine-tuning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first present the multi-objective reward design of RFT, followed by a detailed description of RAPO, to enable the agent to autonomously explore and acquire optimal strategies for both tool interaction and complex reasoning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.1. </span>Reward Design</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a multi-objective reward function, composed of three key components—structural correctness, final answer accuracy, and strategic tool interaction—to effectively guide the TableMind agent in exploring tool-use strategies and enhancing the efficiency of RLVR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
To ensure that the agent produces syntactically valid and parsable reasoning trajectories, we define a format reward <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{format}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>format</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{format}}</annotation></semantics></math> to enforce structural integrity.</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{format}}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if output format is valid},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>format</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if output format is valid</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{format}}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if output format is valid},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A binary reward is assigned based on whether the output adheres to the required structured format (correct usage of &lt;think&gt; and &lt;answer&gt; tags, with a valid JSON object in the final answer).
As the primary indicator of task success, the accuracy reward <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{acc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>acc</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{acc}}</annotation></semantics></math>
directly assesses the correctness of the agent’s final answer against the ground truth. The reward is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{acc}}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{answer matches the ground truth},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>acc</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>answer matches the ground truth</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{acc}}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{answer matches the ground truth},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For Table Question Answering, correctness is measured by exact match with ground-truth answers, while for Table Fact Verification, it is measured by label accuracy. To guide efficient and effective tool use, we introduce an auxiliary reward <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{tool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>tool</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{tool}}</annotation></semantics></math> with an implicit curriculum that encourages exploration early and efficiency later.</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{tool}}=e^{-\rho s}\left(\beta\cdot I_{\mathrm{success}}-C\cdot(N_{\mathrm{turns}})^{2}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>tool</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>success</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>turns</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{tool}}=e^{-\rho s}\left(\beta\cdot I_{\mathrm{success}}-C\cdot(N_{\mathrm{turns}})^{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of global training steps, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function that returns <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if at least one tool call in the trajectory is successful; <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is the positive base reward, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{turns}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>turns</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{turns}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of tool turns, and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a penalty coefficient. The total reward for each reasoning trajectory is the sum of the above components.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.2. </span>RAPO</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce RAPO, a method to improve group-based policy gradient algorithms. It identifies misaligned trajectories—where the model’s confidence ranking disagrees with the true reward—and boosts their learning signal using rank-aware advantage weighting.
The RAPO objective modifies the original GRPO<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> framework and incorporates certain techniques from DAPO<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. First, following recent work<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we removes the KL divergence, allowing the model to no longer be constrained by the reference model and thereby expanding the search space. Second, it employs a Token-Level Policy Gradient Loss to reduce the impact of answer length on the gradient. Finally, it uses the Clip-Higher strategy, which promotes system diversity and prevents entropy collapse. The objective function is formalized as:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J_{\mathrm{RAPO}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>RAPO</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J_{\mathrm{RAPO}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{q,\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}}\bigg[\quad\frac{1}{\sum_{i=1}^{G}|o_{i}|}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}\min\big(r_{i,t}(\theta)A^{\prime}_{i},\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" rspace="1.000em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mi>min</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{q,\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}}\bigg[\quad\frac{1}{\sum_{i=1}^{G}|o_{i}|}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}\min\big(r_{i,t}(\theta)A^{\prime}_{i},\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E4Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{clip}(r_{i,t}(\theta),1-\epsilon_{\mathrm{low}},1+\epsilon_{\mathrm{high}})A^{\prime}_{i}\big)\bigg]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>high</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{clip}(r_{i,t}(\theta),1-\epsilon_{\mathrm{low}},1+\epsilon_{\mathrm{high}})A^{\prime}_{i}\big)\bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability ratio between the current policy <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the old policy <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that was used for data collection, calculated as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{i,t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{i,t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon this framework, we replace the standard group-normalized advantage with the proposed dynamically weighted, rank-aware advantage <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This change ensures that the gradient signal for each token is not only directly proportional to the trajectory’s quality but also explicitly proportional to the degree of misalignment between the model’s confidence and that quality.</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{\prime}_{i}=\gamma_{i}\cdot\frac{R_{i}-\mathrm{mean}(\{R_{j}\}_{j=1}^{G})}{\mathrm{std}(\{R_{j}\}_{j=1}^{G})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{\prime}_{i}=\gamma_{i}\cdot\frac{R_{i}-\mathrm{mean}(\{R_{j}\}_{j=1}^{G})}{\mathrm{std}(\{R_{j}\}_{j=1}^{G})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the base group-normalized advantage multiplied by the rank-aware weighting factor <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. For misaligned trajectories, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The central mechanism of RAPO is its method for identifying and amplifying the learning signal from misaligned trajectory pairs. A misalignment occurs when the model assigns a higher confidence to a low-reward trajectory <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> than to a high-reward trajectory <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> from the same group. This confidence is measured by the log-probability of the sequence, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log P(o_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log P(o_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the length-normalized log-probability of its constituent tokens.
To detect these misalignments, we introduce a pairwise weighting factor <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{w,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{w,l}</annotation></semantics></math>, which acts as a diagnostic flag for each winner loser pair. It checks if the model’s confidence ordering for this specific pair is correct. If the winner’s log-probability does not exceed that of the loser, the pair is considered misaligned, and this factor is elevated to apply extra learning pressure. It is formally defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma_{w,l}=1+\alpha\cdot\mathbb{I}[\log P(o_{w})&lt;\log P(o_{l})]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{w,l}=1+\alpha\cdot\mathbb{I}[\log P(o_{w})&lt;\log P(o_{l})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter controlling the intensity of the re-weighting.
Finally, the comprehensive weighting factor <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for an individual trajectory is computed by averaging these pairwise diagnostic flags from all comparisons involving that trajectory. This dynamic, rank-aware weighting scheme allows RAPO to efficiently identify and correct instances where the model’s confidence is poorly calibrated, leading to a more robust and effective policy optimization process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Inference and Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the inference phase, the model employs an iterative reasoning framework to solve complex problems. This framework operates as a multi-turn cycle, with each cycle comprising the following core steps. Plan: The model first analyzes the user query and the current state to formulate a strategy for the next action. Action: The model generates executable Python code to query or process tabular data. Execution: A code interpreter securely executes the code and returns the output. Reflection: The model receives the execution result as an observation. It then reflects on this outcome to assess progress, verify correctness, and decide whether to generate the final answer or proceed to the next cycle. This plan-action-reflect loop enables the model to perform multi-step reasoning, leverage intermediate results, and self-correct. The process terminates when the model confirms in its reflection step that a solution has been found, or when a predefined maximum number of cycles is reached. Finally, the model synthesizes all information gathered throughout the cycles to generate the final answer. To evaluate model performance, we use the exact match for table question answering tasks and use accuracy for table fact verification tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Main results (%) of different baselines over the benchmarks. In each column, the best result is marked in bold and the second-best result is underlined, while the relative improvement of TableMind over the runner-up is recorded in parentheses.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  Method</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  Models</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  Wikitq</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  TabMWP</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  TabFact</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;" rowspan="3">  <span id="S4.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Training-free</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  Tab-CoT (2023)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin and Lu, 2023</a>)</cite></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  60.43</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  92.21</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  79.05</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  PoTable (2024)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2024</a>)</cite></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  69.56</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  95.76</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  <span id="S4.T1.2.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">88.93</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  Chain-of-Table (2024)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  68.31</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  <span id="S4.T1.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">96.17</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;">  87.78</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:15.0pt;padding-right:15.0pt;" rowspan="5">  <span id="S4.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Tuning-based</span></td>
