<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we outline the experimental setup of TableMind, including datasets, baselines, and evaluation metrics, followed by an analysis of the results to evaluate its effectiveness.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate TableMind on three widely used public table reasoning benchmarks: <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WikiTQ<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pasupat and Liang, 2015</a><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TabMWP<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2022</a><span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span>, and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TabFact</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.
WikiTQ focuses on open-domain question answering, where models generate concise lists of short answers by combining information from Wikipedia tables with their related textual context. TabMWP targets mathematical reasoning over tables, involving multi-step numerical calculations that require precise intermediate computation and reasoning over quantitative relationships. TabFact addresses fact verification, determining whether a textual statement is supported or refuted by the structured content of a given Wikipedia table.
For training, we construct a mixed dataset by randomly sampling 3,500 instances each from the WikiTQ and TabFact training sets, and 1,000 from TabMWP. All evaluations are conducted on the test sets of the respective datasets to ensure consistency and fair comparison with prior work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We choose six strong LLM-based methods as baselines for evaluating three table reasoning tasks<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tab-CoT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin and Lu, 2023</a><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> proposes a method that prompts a large language model to generate a table that models a complex reasoning process in a structured, step-by-step manner. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PoTable<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2024</a><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> employs a plan-then-execute two-stage framework, where an LLM first generates a complete, executable plan, which is then strictly followed by external tools to solve the tabular task. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain-of-Table<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> introduces a paradigm centered on Evolving Tables, where the table is updated after each reasoning step and serves as the input for the subsequent operation, thus evolving throughout the reasoning chain. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TableLlama<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> fine-tunes the Llama 2 model on a diverse table-instruction dataset named TableInstruct, enabling it to handle complex table tasks without requiring a special model architecture.
<span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TableGPT2<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2024</a><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> introduces an innovative semantic table encoder to capture table structure and content, and utilizes large-scale data for pre-training and fine-tuning to achieve deep understanding and processing of tabular data. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Table-R1<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(</span><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a><span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">)</span></cite></span> is a reasoning model trained specifically for table tasks using reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, to enable effective inference-time scaling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3. </span>Implementation Details.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For TableMind, we adopt Qwen3-8B as the backbone model. During the supervised fine-tuning stage, we carefully train on 200 synthetic samples with a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> for one epoch. In the RFT stage, we explicitly implement the RAPO algorithm using the Verl framework, with vLLM consistently employed for generation. In Eq. (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4.4.1. Reward Design ‣ 4.4. Reinforcement Fine-tuning ‣ 4. TableMind ‣ TableMind: An Autonomous Programmatic Agent for Tool-Augmented Table Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), the hyperparameters are set as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and the group size <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=8</annotation></semantics></math>. The batch size is 128, the learning rate is <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, the policy temperature is 1, the maximum tool-calling turns per prompt is 3, and the maximum response length for each turn is 2048 tokens. Both training stages are extensively conducted on a 4-GPU A800 cluster.
To ensure a fair comparison of generation performance, we standardize the number of samples to 1 across all baselines. For other parameters, we faithfully preserve the original configurations proposed by each method, given that the approaches involve distinct target operations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Table Reasoning Performance</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the efficacy of TableMind, we conduct a comprehensive comparison against competitive baselines across three diverse benchmarks: the open-domain question answering dataset Wikitq, the tabular mathematical reasoning dataset TabMWP, and the fact verification dataset TabFact. The quantitative results, summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.5. Inference and Evaluation ‣ 4. TableMind ‣ TableMind: An Autonomous Programmatic Agent for Tool-Augmented Table Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, demonstrate that TableMind achieves state-of-the-art performance, consistently surpassing all other models. In particular, it achieves notable improvements on all three benchmarks compared with the strongest respective baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike Tab-CoT, which relies solely on textual reasoning, PoTable and Chain-of-Table leverage external tools. Offloading deterministic tasks like calculations to these reliable executors minimizes factual errors and directly accounts for their superior performance and robustness. While TableLlama and TableGPT2 are trained with SFT to imitate static reasoning paths, Table-R1 uses a more dynamic RFT framework. This approach allows the model to learn by exploring diverse strategies with reliable feedback, fostering a more robust reasoning capability that surpasses the pattern-matching limitations of SFT and leads to its superior performance. Our approach, TableMind, unifies the strengths of both tool-augmented execution and advanced training paradigms. We leverage external tools for high-fidelity, deterministic operations while concurrently training the model with a RLVR framework. This dual strategy ensures both the factual accuracy of individual steps and the cultivation of a robust, generalizable reasoning capability, allowing our model to surpass prior methods that focus on only one of these aspects.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06278v1/lineChart.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/90;" width="476" height="90" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Analysis of Reward Curves for Key Model Components. (a) SFT initialization provides a significant advantage over Pure RL. (b) Our RAPO algorithm outperforms the GRPO baseline. (c) An instruct-tuned model learns more effectively than a base model. (d) Reward scores improve consistently with model scaling.</figcaption>
</figure>
